# Barkeep

A small, offline, Catima-inspired loyalty/membership card wallet built with
Rust, GTK4, and libadwaita — adaptive from the ground up for phone-sized
screens under Phosh (works fine on desktop too).

## What it does (v1)

- Add / edit / delete cards, with a **live barcode/QR preview** as you type
  the code value and change the format — makes it easy to eyeball-match a
  physical card's symbology to the right dropdown entry
- All **13 barcode/QR formats Catima supports**: QR Code, Code 128,
  Code 39, Code 93, Codabar, EAN-8, EAN-13, ITF, PDF417, UPC-A, UPC-E,
  Aztec, and Data Matrix — or a plain photo fallback for cards with no
  scannable code
- **"+" opens a menu**: Scan with Camera / Scan from Image / Enter Manually
  (Camera is greyed out if no camera is detected)
- **Scan from Image**: pick any image file and Barkeep will find and
  decode a barcode/QR in it automatically, pre-filling the add-card form
- **Import from Catima**: reads a Catima `.zip` or `.csv` backup directly
  (see confidence notes below)
- Color-code cards, search by name or notes
- Barkeep's own import/export as a single portable JSON file

Data lives at `$XDG_DATA_HOME/barkeep/cards.json`
(usually `~/.local/share/barkeep/cards.json`), and nothing ever touches
the network — same "offline by design" philosophy as Catima.

## Layout / adaptiveness

`AdwNavigationSplitView` with an `AdwBreakpoint` at 600sp: collapses to a
single pane on phone width, splits side-by-side on anything wider.

## Building

```bash
sudo apt install build-essential libgtk-4-dev libadwaita-1-dev \
    librsvg2-common pkg-config \
    libgstreamer1.0-dev libgstreamer-plugins-base1.0-dev \
    gstreamer1.0-plugins-good gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad \
    gstreamer1.0-gtk4 gstreamer1.0-pipewire

cargo build --release
./target/release/barkeep
```

## Honest confidence notes

I built this without a working GTK4/libadwaita/GStreamer toolchain or a
camera to test against, so treat the pieces below with different levels
of trust:

**Solid — straightforward Rust, low risk:**
- Card model, JSON store, import/export, search, color-coding
- The adaptive split-view layout
- The Catima CSV parser itself (built against the
  [documented Catima export schema](https://github.com/CatimaLoyalty/Android/wiki/Export-format),
  including the real card-table column order) — groups and
  star/archive/balance metadata are intentionally dropped since Barkeep
  has no equivalent concept yet. Password-protected exports aren't
  supported; export without a password first.

**Probably fine, worth a careful first look:**
- `barcode.rs` encode/decode via the `rxing` crate — I used my best guess
  at current method names (`BitMatrix::width()/height()/get()`,
  `Luma8LuminanceSource::new(...)`, etc.). If `cargo build` complains here,
  check docs.rs/rxing — it should be a handful of renamed calls, not a
  redesign.
- The live preview and "Scan from Image" flows, which lean on the same
  `barcode.rs` functions.

**Genuinely experimental — this is the hard part, even for Catima itself:**
- `camera.rs`. Live camera capture on Linux phones is notoriously
  device-specific (it's why the dedicated
  [Megapixels](https://gitlab.com/postmarketOS/megapixels) project exists
  just for camera capture on PinePhone/Librem5-class hardware). I built a
  GStreamer pipeline using `pipewiresrc`, which is the modern default on
  most current Phosh images, feeding one branch to a live preview
  (`gtk4paintablesink`, from the `gstreamer1.0-gtk4` package) and another
  to a frame grabber that runs barcode detection on each frame. If the
  camera doesn't come up on your device, the first thing to try is
  swapping `CAMERA_SOURCE` in `camera.rs` for `"libcamerasrc"` or
  `"v4l2src"`. The "grey out if no camera" check is a cheap `/dev/video*`
  heuristic, not a real capability probe.

None of this is a reason not to try it — just don't be surprised if the
camera path needs a debugging session on your actual device, while
everything else should mostly just work (or need only small fixes).

## Ideas for v2

- Front/back photo import from Catima exports (currently only the text
  fields and barcode come across, not the stored images)
- Tags and expiry dates
- Home-screen shortcuts for individual cards (like Catima's)
